{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1538560348,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNjU3NjcxMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2888#issuecomment-426576713"},"message":"I'm actually quite sure we used `rev-parse` (may be in a different function) before and there was a reason to switch. I remember, that we generally got into trouble with `.git` either being a symlink or a \".git-file\" in submodules, since git itself failed to fully support both (which it is supposed to and joey complained). Not sure whether this is still the case.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1538574771,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNjY0NTY1MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2888#issuecomment-426645651"},"message":"\u003e I'm actually quite sure we used rev-parse (may be in a different function)\n\nYes, must have been a different function.  `get_git_repo` has remained nearly unchanged since its introduction in f880d6688 (ENH: Finishing --create option for install, 2016-03-08).\n\n`git log -S'-git-dir' -- datalad/support/{annex,git}repo.py` point to\n6dc4c31f5 (BF: get_toppath didn't actually work in direct mode yet, 2017-06-01) and eeedc728a (BF: get_toppath used wrong Runner class and therefore system-git instead of bundled one BF: older git versions don't support git-rev-parse's option --absolute-git-dir, 2017-06-06).\n\nThose probably aren't what you're thinking of though because neither of those avoid `--git-dir`.  Perhaps the issue was discussed in a PR/issue but never made its way into master.\n\n\u003e  I remember, that we generally got into trouble with .git either being a symlink or a \".git-file\" in submodules,\n\nOK.  Quick testing on my end (git v2.19.0) suggests `git rev-parse --git-dir` identifies what (I think) is a reasonable git directory in all three submodule cases (submodule as gitfile, as symlink, and plain .git/), but perhaps older versions of git don't.\n\nAnyway, there's no reason to change something that's working and risk introducing issues.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1538574771,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTg4MTk2MTM2Ng=="},"status":2}]}